# The startup budget counts the pages parked on the kernel's per-CPU free lists (port of GLM 0119,
# MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold#149). On a unified GPU (the GB10) the native engine sizes the
# weights check and the --parallel streams' admission from MemAvailable when a rank starts. On kernel 6.7+ (6.17 on
# the GB10) a bulk free (an image copy, docker save | load, a compiler's exit, the first start of a new image) leaves
# 2-9 GiB of free pages on the per-CPU lists for a minute or two; the kernel drains them when an allocation needs them,
# but neither MemFree nor MemAvailable counts them, so the first start after an image load budgeted several GiB too
# little (here: a fixed --parallel 8 refused with error.TooMany, or a failed weights check).
# native/cuda_memory.zig parkedBytes() sums the ``count:`` lines under each zone's ``pagesets`` in /proc/zoneinfo less
# their ``high_min:`` lines (the level they hold at rest; 512 MiB when a kernel prints none), so a warm start's budget
# does not move; 0 on anything unparsable, never an error. native/cuda.zig pool() reads /proc/meminfo first and
# /proc/zoneinfo right after (pages that drain in between are undercounted, never counted twice), adds the excess to
# MemAvailable capped at MemTotal, and logs it from 0.25 GiB. Unified GPUs only; the 10 % reserve is unchanged.
# No switch: sizing only, the same replies. Tests: GLM's tools/parked_pages_check.py cases translated
# (zig test zig/src/native/cuda_memory.zig, 9 pass) and a Linux-only test on the host's own /proc/zoneinfo. Part of
# every port image run on the pair (every exact.py run identical to the daily baseline). The recipe's own start check
# (scripts/memavail.sh) counts the same pages.
# By Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo). Apache-2.0.
